[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Freestyle vs. Pipeline Projects and Job Configuration in Jenkins"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jenkins is a foundational tool in CI/CD pipelines. When setting up CI/CD jobs, administrators and developers primarily choose between two project types: Freestyle Projects and Pipeline Projects. Understanding their differences and configuration nuances is critical for scalable automation."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Freestyle Projects"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Freestyle projects are the traditional, GUI-driven job type in Jenkins. They offer a straightforward, step-by-step form-based interface where users define source code management, triggers, build steps, and post-build actions by checking boxes and filling out text fields."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Easy to set up for beginners, highly intuitive GUI, excellent for simple and linear build tasks."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Difficult to scale for complex workflows, lacks version control for the job configuration itself, and does not support parallel execution natively without heavy plugin reliance."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Pipeline Projects"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pipeline projects define the entire build process as code using Groovy-based Domain Specific Language (DSL). This is known as Jenkins Pipeline. The configuration is typically stored in a text file named Jenkinsfile, which resides in the root of the source code repository."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Configuration-as-Code (Git-tracked), supports complex multi-branch workflows, built-in support for parallel execution, and pauses for manual approvals."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Steeper learning curve requiring knowledge of Groovy syntax, debugging can be complex."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Basic Declarative Pipeline Configuration"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is an example of a standard declarative Jenkinsfile used in Pipeline projects. This defines a multi-stage process involving building, testing, and deployment."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                echo 'Building the application...'
                sh 'mvn clean package'
            }
        }
        stage('Test') {
            steps {
                echo 'Running unit tests...'
                sh 'mvn test'
            }
        }
        stage('Deploy') {
            steps {
                echo 'Deploying to staging environment...'
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Step-by-Step Job Configuration Guide"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"To configure a Pipeline project in the Jenkins dashboard, follow these procedural steps:"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 1: Open your Jenkins dashboard and click on "New Item" in the left-hand menu."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 2: Enter an item name (e.g., "My-Pipeline-Job") and select "Pipeline", then click "OK"."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 3: In the job configuration page, navigate to the "Pipeline" section at the bottom."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 4: Under the Definition dropdown, choose "Pipeline script from SCM" if you are loading the Jenkinsfile from a Git repository."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 5: Select "Git", supply your repository URL, specify credentials if required, and ensure the script path points to "Jenkinsfile"."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 6: Click "Save" to finalize the job setup and trigger your first build."}]
